From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: No heap allocation for each SCTP packet or DTLS record

At full rate a data channel allocated and freed, for every packet, three buffers of 1 to 2 KiB (usrsctp's linear
copy for the write callback and its mbuf cluster, SctpTransport's message of the packet, DtlsTransport's message of
the record) and two or three mbufs. musl's allocator maps groups of the larger sizes whole and unmaps them when they
empty, so a fifth of the CPU went to mmap and munmap. usrsctp now copies a packet into one buffer per thread (the
callback copies what it keeps before it returns) and keeps freed mbufs (up to 512) and clusters (up to 256).
SctpTransport reuses its packet message when no layer kept it, and the records a burst sent come back to their
transport for the next ones.
